  "question": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\qt0040_0db7c5363fad.png\" style=\"width: 650px; height: 122px;\"/></p><p>The total number of hydrogen atoms in product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>A</mi></math> and product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> is__________.</p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "10",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>When bromoethane reacts with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi></math> in the presence of ethyl alcohol gives Ethene. </p><p>Similarly When bromoethane reacts with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi></math> in the aqueous medium gives Ethyl alcohol. This reaction is an example of a nucleophilic substitution reaction.</p><p>The reaction is as follows,</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0040_9571568a396f.png\" style=\"width: 700px; height: 118px;\"/></p><p>Total number of hydrogen atom in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> that is Ethene and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> that is  Ethyl alcohol is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn></math></p>"
  }
